1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
Hello English learners.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:06,600
Welcome back to another podcast here with us at EnglishPod.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:07,040
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:07,880
And I'm Erica.

5
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:15,920
And today we have an advanced lesson for all of you and a very good one because we're going to be talking about a very serious topic, global warming.

6
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:16,760
That's right.

7
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:28,240
And you know, even though most people agree that global warming is a big problem, there's still this sort of fringe element of people who believe that actually global warming doesn't exist.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:28,420
Exactly.

9
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:30,520
And that's what we're going to be looking at today.

10
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:37,800
But before we tell you more or less what the dialogue is about, let's take a look at some words in vocabulary preview.

11
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,440
Vocabulary preview.

12
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:43,840
We're going to look at two words here and you'll hear both of these in the dialogue.

13
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,000
So the first one is polar ice caps.

14
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:48,780
Polar ice caps.

15
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:50,400
Polar ice caps.

16
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,880
So when we talk about polar ice caps, polar is...

17
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:53,280
Like the poles.

18
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:54,880
The poles, right?

19
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:56,200
The north and south poles of the earth.

20
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:56,380
Okay.

21
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:57,160
So the extreme...

22
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,100
The top and bottom.

23
00:00:59,200 --> 00:00:59,660
The top and bottom of the planet.

24
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:01,200
Yep.

25
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:02,200
Okay.

26
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:03,200
And what about ice caps?

27
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:11,880
So the polar ice caps are masses of ice that live or that exist on the top and the bottom of the world.

28
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:15,030
We have these big chunks of ice that are on the top at the bottom of the planet.

29
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:15,430
Yes.

30
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:16,510
They're there all year round.

31
00:01:16,770 --> 00:01:17,090
Okay.

32
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:18,970
So these are the polar ice caps.

33
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:20,190
What about our next word?

34
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:22,870
Well, next word is endangered species.

35
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,300
An endangered species.

36
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:26,880
An endangered species.

37
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:27,230
Okay.

38
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:29,600
So what is an endangered species?

39
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:34,150
It's a type of animal that is at risk of completely dying out.

40
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:34,820
Okay.

41
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:36,780
So it's not completely extinct.

42
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:37,520
Right.

43
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:38,800
So they're still...

44
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:40,160
It's in danger.

45
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:41,160
Okay.

46
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:42,160
In danger of.

47
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:43,160
Yeah.

48
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:44,760
So an example of endangered species would be...

49
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:45,740
The Bengal tigers.

50
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:46,400
Okay.

51
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:48,300
The tigers or the pandas.

52
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:49,100
Right.

53
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:50,840
Or maybe gorillas.

54
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:51,080
Right.

55
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:54,700
So all of these animals, there are very few of them left in the world.

56
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:56,260
So they are endangered species.

57
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:57,100
Okay.

58
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,700
So with that, I think we're just about ready to turn to our dialogue.

59
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:10,520
Now here we're going to learn a lot of language that you can use when you want to make an argument or express your point of view in a very sort of academic way.

60
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:12,200
But Marco, what are we arguing about here?

61
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:20,080
So we're going to listen as a college professor is lecturing about global warming and well, one of the students doesn't really agree with what he's saying.

62
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:36,080
And therefore, global warming is the greatest deception of the early 21st century.

63
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:36,720
Questions?

64
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:38,720
Yeah.

65
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:43,440
In the lecture you said there's more evidence in the scientific record supporting global cooling.

66
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:44,240
Well, yes.

67
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,440
Essentially, the historical record supports a theory of climate cycles.

68
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:52,300
Warming and cooling are cooperating processes in the planetary ecosystem.

69
00:02:52,740 --> 00:03:00,300
If that's true and the planet is getting cooler, what explains the rapid melting of the polar ice caps and the dramatic rise in the global average temperatures?

70
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:04,240
That are global temperatures rising.

71
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:06,610
If you look at the data from 1975, you'll-

72
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:07,430
You'll be misled.

73
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:11,870
If you were serious, you would look at the record starting in the 1880s.

74
00:03:12,070 --> 00:03:15,130
Then you would see how dramatically the Earth's temperature has changed.

75
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:17,540
Young lady, I beg to differ.

76
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:21,760
Look, the point of the lecture was to emphasize that there's evidence for both sides.

77
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:26,620
And I'm putting forth the argument that there's greater evidence in support of the global cooling hypothesis.

78
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:34,960
Look, it's an indisputable fact that the public is being manipulated and scared into believing that there's some kind of climate crisis.

79
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:38,680
This scaremongering is done quite simply for political reasons.

80
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:47,240
But even without the uncomfortable reality that greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide contribute to global warming, isn't the topic appropriate for politicians to discuss?

81
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:53,220
Not if they want to use your tax dollars and mine to fund completely unnecessary initiatives.

82
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:58,460
Yeah, like conservation, protecting endangered species, and investing in renewable energy.

83
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:04,080
At the very least, you have to concede that this debate has the potential to end our dependence on foreign oil.

84
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:09,240
Buying oil supports autocratic countries that use these revenues to devastating ends.

85
00:04:09,980 --> 00:04:13,280
I've never been so disrespected in all of my days.

86
00:04:13,930 --> 00:04:17,110
I'm a professor, a scientist, and a researcher of high regard.

87
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:19,550
Yeah, and a duplicitous one at that.

88
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,370
Everyone knows you're in the pocket of the oil lobby.

89
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,810
Why should we trust your so-called findings more than tobacco institute studies, which say smoking doesn't harm health?

90
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:29,970
You're full of it.

91
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:32,710
Some people just cannot handle civil debate.

92
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:46,840
All right, so apparently this college professor is not very truthful in what he's saying, right?

93
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:51,440
Well, yeah, maybe he believes it, but they certainly have very strong and differing opinions.

94
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:57,820
So that's the interesting part is that we heard a lot of language that she used to argue her point of view.

95
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:01,760
So this is interesting and we can start now looking at some of this language in Language Takeaway.

96
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:04,810
Language Takeaway.

97
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:13,460
So the college professor was arguing against this idea of global warming and she said, look, it's an indisputable fact.

98
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:16,680
Okay, an indisputable fact.

99
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:17,960
An indisputable fact.

100
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:21,760
Okay, so let's break it down and let's take a look at this word, indisputable.

101
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:23,880
You can't argue against it.

102
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:29,040
Okay, so something indisputable is you can't argue with it and will affect something that's true.

103
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:32,740
Okay, so you can't argue with this piece of information.

104
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:33,840
Yeah.

105
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:34,840
All right.

106
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:35,840
Indisputable fact.

107
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:36,510
So why don't we give a few examples?

108
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:42,170
It's an indisputable fact that China is the most populous nation on earth.

109
00:05:42,550 --> 00:05:43,450
Okay, exactly.

110
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:49,000
Or it's an indisputable fact that EnglishPod is the best podcast out there.

111
00:05:49,010 --> 00:05:50,480
Indeed, it is indisputable.

112
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:51,230
All right.

113
00:05:51,490 --> 00:05:54,530
So indisputable fact and what about our next word?

114
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:58,970
Well, the student accused the professor of scaremongering.

115
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:00,040
Scaremongering.

116
00:06:01,090 --> 00:06:01,590
Scaremongering.

117
00:06:01,930 --> 00:06:05,210
Okay, now this is kind of a strange word, scaremongering.

118
00:06:05,490 --> 00:06:09,210
Yeah, and I think some examples will help us to understand the meaning a little bit better.

119
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:11,270
Example one.

120
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:18,310
Software flaws are not the same as virus attacks and saying otherwise is just scaremongering.

121
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:20,980
Example two.

122
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:26,750
There's been a lot of scaremongering caused by the rumor that mobile phones cause cancer.

123
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:33,270
Okay, so basically we understand that it's kind of putting fear in people's minds.

124
00:06:33,630 --> 00:06:36,570
Yeah, it's sort of like selling fear for your own benefit.

125
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:41,550
Okay, so making people afraid for some objective or some purpose.

126
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:45,670
Yeah, and you know, this word reminds me of the word a fishmonger.

127
00:06:45,990 --> 00:06:47,310
Okay, a fishmonger.

128
00:06:47,410 --> 00:06:47,610
What's that?

129
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:49,730
Which is basically a seller of fish.

130
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:53,470
So a scaremonger is a seller of fear.

131
00:06:53,750 --> 00:06:54,490
Okay, interesting.

132
00:06:54,810 --> 00:06:56,390
So now we have a way of remembering it.

133
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:56,670
Yes.

134
00:06:57,170 --> 00:06:57,650
Scaremongering.

135
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:01,600
All right, and moving on to our next word, we have autocratic.

136
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:02,880
Autocratic.

137
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:04,820
An autocratic country.

138
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:10,820
So an autocratic country is ruled by someone who holds total power.

139
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:12,940
Okay, so we have an autocratic country.

140
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:16,480
So basically we have a leader that rules everything, right?

141
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:18,160
Right.

142
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:21,400
So if we apply it not only to a country, we can say a person is autocratic.

143
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:25,240
Yeah, you could say, I can't stand my boss's autocratic leadership style.

144
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:26,560
Or maybe a company.

145
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:30,450
This company is a very autocratic organization.

146
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:31,670
Okay, very good.

147
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:33,430
And what about our last word?

148
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:37,520
The student accused the professor of being duplicitous.

149
00:07:38,070 --> 00:07:41,000
Okay, so she said he is duplicitous.

150
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:42,000
Yes, duplicitous.

151
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,160
All right, what does that mean?

152
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:50,990
When you are duplicitous, you have behavior that is dishonest and is designed to trick people.

153
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:54,400
Okay, so you're not a very honest person if you're duplicitous.

154
00:07:54,690 --> 00:07:58,870
Yeah, you're telling lies as a means of making people do something or tricking them.

155
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:00,050
Okay, very good.

156
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:03,630
So a duplicitous person or a duplicitous idea maybe?

157
00:08:04,390 --> 00:08:04,750
Okay.

158
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:08,610
All right, so these are all the words that we have on language takeaway today.

159
00:08:08,970 --> 00:08:16,090
But now let's move on to some very interesting phrases that we can use to put forward some arguments in Fluency Builder.

160
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:18,640
Fluency Builder.

161
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:25,640
Okay, and Marco, you actually just mentioned our first phrase, which is I'm putting forth the argument that.

162
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:28,440
Okay, so putting forth the argument that.

163
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,080
So if you put forth an argument.

164
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:32,160
You present it.

165
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,260
You present it or you push it towards somebody.

166
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:34,880
Yeah, you give it.

167
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:38,840
So can we just put forth an argument?

168
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:42,480
Well actually you can put forth a lot of things, like put forth a suggestion.

169
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:45,760
Okay, I'm putting forth a suggestion that we should get a new computer.

170
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:46,560
Or an idea.

171
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:49,640
Tim put forth the idea that we should go on vacation to Hawaii.

172
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:50,660
Oh, good idea.

173
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:54,220
All right, so you can put forth many different things.

174
00:08:54,220 --> 00:09:04,220
So the professor obviously is putting forth the argument that global warming is a myth and the student obviously totally disagreed and she said you have to concede that.

175
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:07,260
Okay, you have to concede that.

176
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:09,520
You have to concede that.

177
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:14,440
All right, so this word concede or you have to concede.

178
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:17,320
Why don't we listen to some examples before we listen to the meaning.

179
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,560
Example one.

180
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:26,050
I'm sorry, but I can't concede with you on this point.

181
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:28,600
Example two.

182
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:33,320
Julie conceded that she might have forgotten her sister's birthday.

183
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:36,600
Example three.

184
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:39,910
Both philosophers conceded that their philosophies have some errors.

185
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:46,260
Okay, so you have to concede that.

186
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:51,100
Basically you have to agree that this one point in my argument is true.

187
00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:57,820
Maybe you're arguing many different points with somebody but you tell them, well, you have to concede that the earth is round.

188
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:00,640
Okay, so that part I can't argue with.

189
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:01,260
It's really true.

190
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:02,380
Okay, very good.

191
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:03,160
So concede.

192
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:11,600
Now, at the end of the dialogue, the student accused the professor of being in the pocket of the oil lobby.

193
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:14,790
Okay, so he's in the pocket of.

194
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:16,170
To be in the pocket of.

195
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:19,640
All right, so if I have somebody in my pocket, what does that mean?

196
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:21,230
It means basically you're bribing them.

197
00:10:21,550 --> 00:10:24,030
Okay, so maybe I have the senator in my pocket.

198
00:10:24,330 --> 00:10:26,930
Okay, so obviously you're paying the senator to do what you want.

199
00:10:27,110 --> 00:10:27,830
Okay, very good.

200
00:10:28,050 --> 00:10:33,950
So it's another way, it's a very informal way of saying I'm bribing somebody or that person is being bribed.

201
00:10:34,270 --> 00:10:34,470
That's right.

202
00:10:35,030 --> 00:10:37,150
So why don't we move to our final phrase.

203
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:42,960
The student was talking about the professor's so-called findings.

204
00:10:42,960 --> 00:10:47,200
Okay, so your so-called findings.

205
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:48,200
So-called.

206
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:50,320
So this word so-called.

207
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:54,240
You use this word when you want to take away from the importance of an idea.

208
00:10:55,180 --> 00:10:56,980
All right, so I use it before a noun, right?

209
00:10:57,400 --> 00:11:00,240
Okay, so for example, my so-called friends.

210
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:03,240
Okay, so these friends are not really your friends maybe.

211
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:06,400
So yeah, they're friends in name but they don't act like friends.

212
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:09,580
Okay, so you can say my so-called job.

213
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:13,080
Okay, so maybe you kind of go to work but do nothing all day.

214
00:11:13,220 --> 00:11:15,660
Right, or maybe you don't even really like what you do.

215
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:16,000
Right.

216
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:21,200
Right, so my so-called job, my so-called friends or maybe my so-called teacher.

217
00:11:21,570 --> 00:11:22,670
Okay, so this is a bad teacher.

218
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:24,360
Exactly.

219
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:26,730
So we use it a lot with many different nouns, right?

220
00:11:27,110 --> 00:11:27,470
That's right.

221
00:11:27,690 --> 00:11:30,530
So that's our last phrase for fluency builder.

222
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:48,760
Now I think it'll help us to understand these words and phrases a little bit better to hear them again in context.

223
00:11:48,760 --> 00:11:52,940
And therefore global warming is the greatest deception of the early 21st century.

224
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:53,580
Questions?

225
00:11:55,390 --> 00:12:00,330
Yeah, in the lecture you said there's more evidence in the scientific record supporting global cooling?

226
00:12:00,710 --> 00:12:01,130
Well, yes.

227
00:12:02,090 --> 00:12:05,310
Essentially, the historical record supports a theory of climate cycles.

228
00:12:06,030 --> 00:12:09,170
Warming and cooling are cooperating processes in the planetary ecosystem.

229
00:12:09,630 --> 00:12:17,270
If that's true and the planet is getting cooler, what explains the rapid melting of the polar ice caps and the dramatic rise in the global average temperatures?

230
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:21,120
But are global temperatures rising?

231
00:12:21,130 --> 00:12:23,490
If you look at the data from 1975, you'll...

232
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:24,310
You'll be misled.

233
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:28,750
If you were serious, you would look at the record starting in the 1880s.

234
00:12:28,930 --> 00:12:32,010
Then you would see how dramatically the Earth's temperature has changed.

235
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:34,400
Young lady, I beg to differ.

236
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:39,120
Look, the point of the lecture was to emphasize that there is evidence for both sides.

237
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:43,540
And I'm putting forth the argument that there's greater evidence in support of the global cooling hypothesis.

238
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:51,840
Look, it's an indisputable fact that the public is being manipulated and scared into believing that there's some kind of climate crisis.

239
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:55,540
This scaremongering is done quite simply for political reasons.

240
00:12:56,010 --> 00:13:04,110
But even without the uncomfortable reality that greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide contribute to global warming, isn't the topic appropriate for politicians to discuss?

241
00:13:04,790 --> 00:13:10,170
Not if they want to use your tax dollars and mine to fund completely unnecessary initiatives.

242
00:13:10,470 --> 00:13:15,330
Yeah, like conservation, protecting endangered species, and investing in renewable energy.

243
00:13:15,810 --> 00:13:20,950
At the very least, you have to concede that this debate has the potential to end our dependence on foreign oil.

244
00:13:21,610 --> 00:13:26,130
Buying oil supports autocratic countries that use these revenues to devastating ends.

245
00:13:26,810 --> 00:13:30,170
I've never been so disrespected in all of my days.

246
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:33,990
I'm a professor, a scientist, and a researcher of high regard.

247
00:13:34,430 --> 00:13:36,430
Yeah, and a duplicitous one at that.

248
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:39,250
Everyone knows you're in the pocket of the oil lobby.

249
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:45,690
Why should we trust your so-called findings more than tobacco institute studies, which say smoking doesn't harm health?

250
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:46,850
You're full of it.

251
00:13:47,510 --> 00:13:49,590
Some people just cannot handle civil debate.

252
00:13:58,720 --> 00:13:59,800
All right, very good.

253
00:13:59,850 --> 00:14:02,770
So interesting ways of putting forth arguments.

254
00:14:02,770 --> 00:14:06,470
And well, it's an interesting topic and very controversial, right?

255
00:14:06,870 --> 00:14:07,330
That's right.

256
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:16,440
But what I noticed in this dialogue is that the student was pretty assertive in putting forth her argument and standing up to the teacher.

257
00:14:16,470 --> 00:14:16,670
Right.

258
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:18,440
And she was with the teacher a lot.

259
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:22,200
And this is interesting because it's a very cultural aspect, I think, right?

260
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:23,200
Yeah.

261
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:35,970
In North American universities, there is this idea that it's really important to think critically and to argue against different ideas and to really speak up when you don't agree with something.

262
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:37,840
Right.

263
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:41,630
So basically, there's this culture of questioning, not only what you're being taught, but you can also question the teacher.

264
00:14:41,810 --> 00:14:45,000
You don't actually have to believe everything that the teacher is saying.

265
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:45,680
Exactly.

266
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:48,800
And I think this situation is really interesting.

267
00:14:48,980 --> 00:14:52,920
You know, you always go to these lectures and there's always a question and answer period at the end of the lectures.

268
00:14:53,600 --> 00:15:05,240
And I think that there's like this group of sort of academically minded people who feel like it's really cool to ask these really difficult questions and engage in arguments with the profs.

269
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:06,290
Yeah, yeah, that's really true.

270
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:14,550
But actually, she used a lot of polite language and a lot of polite ways of actually arguing with the professor until the last point when she said, you're full of it.

271
00:15:14,730 --> 00:15:15,910
Yeah, you're full of it.

272
00:15:16,120 --> 00:15:17,120
Right.

273
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:18,120
You're full of it.

274
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:19,120
So what's she saying here?

275
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:20,120
All right.

276
00:15:20,120 --> 00:15:22,880
She's basically saying that you're full of shit.

277
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:24,930
So yeah, you wouldn't really say that.

278
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:28,790
So to be a little bit less rude, you would just say, you're full of it.

279
00:15:29,050 --> 00:15:29,170
Yeah.

280
00:15:29,330 --> 00:15:31,070
But that is one step too far.

281
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:33,880
I think this would be considered a little bit rude.

282
